ign although  he did win  and later i worked for time in the  district government as a consultant  and  it was so it was seeing  various things up close  that i was able to realize that i could  not just attach  i could not attach my endorsement and  support to a politician simply because  he was black  and i think that a number of  african-americans throughout this  country have done that and so  the book which talks about the new age  of black leaders  goes right to that the broader theme of  the book is to  sort of assess  what we as african-americans must do  uh if we are going to really be  politically empowered in this country  and part of it is to objectively  evaluate our leaders not just to  stand behind them and support everything  they do simply because they're black um  and so my independence then comes from  my understanding that uh everything that  everything black is not always gold  i have two final questions 20 30 years  from now  when people look back at the history of  washington dc and marion barry  what will his legacy be  i think the main thing that people will  remember about marion barry is that he  helped  in a very dramatic way to expand the  middle class here  and in uh and and also to explain expand  the middle class and pg county uh prince  george's county which is you know  adjacent to washington dc as a suburb is  now one of the more wealthy and better  educated suburbs in the country and and  predominantly black and many of those  people lived in washington worked for  marion barry worked for the district  government  marion barry did a great deal to to help  to create that middle class  is he going to run again in 1998 and if  he does would you vote for him  i don't expect him to run again in 1998  i think uh that marion barry understands  that with the control board he will have  no power until the year 2002 and  congress may be inclined to strip him of  the last few little agencies that he  currently has power over so i expect him 